KILL(1)                        P32W Unix                         KILL(1)

NAME
       kill - send a signal to processes

SYNOPSIS
       kill [-signal | -s signal] [--] pid ...
       kill -l [n ...]

DESCRIPTION
       kill sends a signal to each process named by its process id,
       as ps(1) shows them. The default signal is TERM (15), which
       ends a process that does not catch or ignore it; KILL (9)
       cannot be caught or ignored.

       The signal is given as a number, 0 to 15, or as a name, with
       or without the SIG prefix, in either case: -9, -KILL, -kill
       and -SIGKILL are the same, as are -s 9 and -s KILL. Signal 0
       (-0) sends nothing but checks that the process exists and may
       be signalled. A pid is a number; -- before the pids is
       allowed.

       An ordinary user may signal only his own processes (those
       whose real uid is his real or effective uid); root may
       signal any.

OPTIONS
       -l     list the signal numbers and names
       -l n   print the name of signal n, or of the signal that
              ended a command whose exit status (from sh(1)) is n,
              129 to 143
       -s signal
              send that signal (as -signal)

       The signals are:

        1 HUP     2 INT     3 QUIT    4 ILL     5 TRAP
        6 ABRT    7 EMT     8 FPE     9 KILL   10 BUS
       11 SEGV   12 SYS    13 PIPE   14 ALRM   15 TERM

       (kill -l prints them eight to a line, separated by tabs.)

EXAMPLES
       kill 23                       ask process 23 to stop
       kill -9 23                    stop it, no matter what
       kill -INT 42                  as if ^C were typed at it
       kill -0 42 && echo alive      does process 42 exist?
       kill -l 137                   KILL: what ended a command

EXIT STATUS
       0 if every signal was sent; 1 if any failed ("kill: 42: no
       such process", "kill: 1: not owner", or "kill: x: not a
       process id"); 2 for a usage error or a signal that is not
       0-15 or a known name ("kill: 99: bad signal").

SEE ALSO
       ps(1), sh(1), top(1), kill(2), signal(2)

BUGS
       There are no process groups: a pid of 0 finds no process
       (no such process), and a negative pid cannot be given.

       A process holds only one pending signal; a second sent
       before it runs replaces the first.
